{"name":"Midas Finger Gold","hex":"#f6b404","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"finger","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slender jointed extremity of the human hand, (often) exclusive of the thumb, and the corresponding part, irrespective of including a thumb, in many other animals.","Similar or similar-looking extremities in other animals, particularly:\n\nThe lower, smaller segment of an arthropod claw.\nOne of the supporting structures of wings in birds, bats, etc. evolved from earlier toes or fingers.\nOne of the slender bony structures before the pectoral fins of gurnards and sea robins (Triglidae)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To identify or point out; to blame for something.","To report to or identify for the authorities; to inform on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/finger"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Midas","blocks":[{"text":"Midas was a king of Phrygia with whom many myths became associated, as well as two later members of the Phrygian royal house."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Red-figure_stamnos_Silenos_Mida_440BC_Cropped.jpg/330px-Red-figure_stamnos_Silenos_Mida_440BC_Cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midas","wikidata":"Q308398"},{"title":"Finger","blocks":[{"text":"A finger is a prominent, multi-jointed digit on the forelimbs of tetrapod vertebrate animals with prehensile extremities such as humans and other primates, and is typically covered with a keratinized nail to protect the end. Most tetrapods have five digits (pentadactyly), digits that are significantly shorter than the metacarpal/metatarsals, are typically referred to as toes; while digits that are notably elongated are called fingers. In humans, the fingers are opposable and have with two or three phalanges with fairly good flexional range of motion, serving as an important organ of tactile sensation and fine movements, which are crucial to the dexterity of the hands and the ability to grasp and manipulate objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Human_fingers_both_sides_2.jpg/330px-Human_fingers_both_sides_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finger","wikidata":"Q620207"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/MidasSehri.Tomb.jpg/500px-MidasSehri.Tomb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/MidasSehri.Tomb.jpg/1280px-MidasSehri.Tomb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/MidasSehri.Tomb.jpg/500px-MidasSehri.Tomb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Midas Monument, a Phrygian rock-cut tomb dedicated to Midas (700 BCE)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMidasSehri.Tomb.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Midas_gold2.jpg/500px-Midas_gold2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Midas_gold2.jpg/500px-Midas_gold2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the Nathaniel Hawthorne version of the Midas myth, Midas's daughter turns to a golden statue when he touches her (illustration by Walter Crane for the 1893 edition).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMidas_gold2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Turkey-1428_%282216632594%29.jpg/500px-Turkey-1428_%282216632594%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Turkey-1428_%282216632594%29.jpg/1280px-Turkey-1428_%282216632594%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Turkey-1428_%282216632594%29.jpg/500px-Turkey-1428_%282216632594%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The \"Tomb of Midas\" in Gordion, dated 740 BCE","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATurkey-1428_(2216632594).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Gordion69.JPG/500px-Gordion69.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail